      <text xml:space="preserve">'''Kálmán Kalocsay''' ([[October 6]], [[1891]]-[[February 27]], [[1976]]), in Hungarian name order '''Kalocsay Kálmán''' ([[International Phonetic Alphabet for English|pronounced]] {{IPA|/'k&amp;#596;lot&amp;#643;ay 'ka:lma:n/}}) is one of the foremost figures in the history of [[Esperanto literature]]. He left a rich legacy to the language and culture of [[Esperanto]] in his original poetry and his translations of literary works from his native [[Hungarian language|Hungarian]] and other languages of Europe.

A surgeon by profession, Kalocsay published his first collection of original poems in [[1921]], ''Mondo kaj Koro'' (''World and Heart''). During the [[1920s]] and [[1930s]], he was the editor of the literary magazine ''Literatura Mondo'' (&quot;Literary World&quot;), which became home to an influential group of authors who came to be collectively known as the Budapest School.

In [[1932]], using the pseudonym of &quot;Peter Peneter&quot;, Kalocsay released a book of erotic verse entitled ''Sekretaj Sonetoj'' (&quot;Secret Sonnets&quot;); perhaps more prosaic but no less remembered was his ''Plena Gramatiko de Esperanto'' (&quot;Complete Grammar of Esperanto&quot;), co-written with [[Gaston Waringhien]] in [[1935]].

