      <text xml:space="preserve">The '''Roundheads''' was the nickname given to supporters of the [[Parliamentarian]] cause in the [[English Civil War]]. The [[Charles I of England|Royalists]], on the other side, were the nicknamed [[Cavaliers]].

The Roundheads were so nicknamed because of the relatively short plain page boy style haircuts favoured by [[Puritan]]s, rather than the long ringlets of the fashionable courtly Cavaliers.

The name started as a term of abuse in the [[New Model Army]], it was a punishable offence to call a fellow [[soldier]] a Roundhead.

